   <worksheet xml:id="conditional-probability">
    <title>Conditional Probability (1.3)</title>
    <page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
    <p>
      What is the probability that in three tosses of a coin,
      exactly <m>2</m> are heads?
    </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
  <statement>
    <p>
    Suppose that you know that at least one of the three tosses
      was a head. Now what is the probability that exactly <m>2</m> were heads?
    </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<definition xml:id="conditional-prob-def">
<title>Conditional Probability(B:1.3-1)</title>
<statement>
    <p>
      The conditional probability of an event <m>A</m>, given that
      event <m>B</m> has occurred, is defined by
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>
</exercise>
    <p>
      Note: If <m>P(B) \gt 0</m>, then
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(A\mid B) \geq 0</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(B\mid B) = 1</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>A_{1},A_{2},...,A_{k}</m> are disjoint, then
            <m>P((A_{1} \cup A_{2} \cup \cdots \cup A_{k}) \mid B) = P(A_{1} \mid B) + P(A_{2} \mid B) + \cdots + P(A_{k} \mid B)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      In other words, <m>P(A\mid B)</m> satisfies all of the rules of a
      probability function.
    </p>
<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Suppose <m>P(A) = 0.5</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.2</m>, and
            <m>P(A\cap B) = 0.15</m>.
</p>
</introduction>
<task workspace="0.25in">
<statement>
    <p>
      Do you expect <m>P(A\mid B)</m> to be less than, greater than,
      or equal to <m>P(A)</m>?
    </p>
</statement>
</task>
<task workspace="0.5in">
